#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f26cd7-b4e2-533f-9258-7e760fd6f507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in L$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369970} {:answer/id #uuid "a664ff91-48f1-5ae4-ada4-3fa4b166eb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369971} {:answer/id #uuid "07f60e7f-6e02-5f1c-b720-366aa10a0075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of functions in $L$ is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369972} {:answer/id #uuid "22a4e1ec-05fc-513a-bbbb-196e2f363402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of functions in $L$ is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369973} {:answer/id #uuid "02790ff3-06cd-5da0-9e4b-98da0a0d0833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a664ff91-48f1-5ae4-ada4-3fa4b166eb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369971} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be1f1ff-185c-5903-841f-0172a0c5e527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15beea94-47dc-575a-be81-e2d3b2547e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106930" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3638f7b7-802a-5b50-aa15-d0eaadee0ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369977} {:answer/id #uuid "cf08fb6b-e3b2-5444-b00f-2d86b3d4ef55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369978} {:answer/id #uuid "85ad6021-1111-585d-ba15-9f99f5bc0e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369979} {:answer/id #uuid "15d8720c-d1e6-54d7-95d2-a4a228ee7476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369980} {:answer/id #uuid "ae72d013-20aa-5576-9391-4efcb759de85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15d8720c-d1e6-54d7-95d2-a4a228ee7476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369980} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e95976d-6212-5c03-ba82-7f260ee5e5e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01cc4fec-23d5-5237-aea5-3e56464042be" :question/math-academy-id "q-106940" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $y$ and $z$ are vectors such that $x+y=z+x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $y=z.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =z+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =z+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =z+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =z &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =z+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =z+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =z+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =z &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33bc630d-d0c0-5066-b1ce-17330dcdabf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of matrices do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369984} {:answer/id #uuid "531c2d53-e212-5909-8ba9-484863df4a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot A\\ne A$ for all $A\\in K$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369985} {:answer/id #uuid "31989510-9ee3-533e-ba1e-dbaada39a136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369986} {:answer/id #uuid "e822e1fc-9467-5db2-b2b9-2e54f6438e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of matrices is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369987} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1eb1df-2deb-5d1e-ad76-5ace4dce98f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of matrices is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31989510-9ee3-533e-ba1e-dbaada39a136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369986} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec4fdc0-c432-52d0-af9b-88539b8e2fc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e9325bc-24c1-5d82-b1cc-1e1fb76a771a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94807" :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all $2\\times 1$ matrices $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ such that $x,y\\in R$ and $x+y=1.$  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because **the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K.$** *  \n\nIndeed, adding the entries of the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix yields $0+0=0\\ne 1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- addition of matrices is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- addition and scalar multiplication of matrices  satisfy the distributive laws. $✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot A=A$ for all $A\\in K.$ $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea792ce-4a6e-5721-9a18-f99292949277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition in $Y$ is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369991} {:answer/id #uuid "dc50024b-9085-5119-8f2d-a8ca14bd95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of $(x,y)\\in Y$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369992} {:answer/id #uuid "93950d71-49ab-5d0d-979c-95c7a0e6b532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ does not contain the zero element" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369993} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f8dea0-a011-58db-a415-7ee98ad9d04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication in $Y$  do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369994} {:answer/id #uuid "171a1a9b-b707-5744-b4d1-6b9dfc2d920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition in $Y$ is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc50024b-9085-5119-8f2d-a8ca14bd95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplication of $(x,y)\\in Y$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369992} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c74e80-66a0-5e58-b9e1-e1d5d2a42b4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96e3d27f-a6f3-58f8-a748-ec53b79d0713" :question/math-academy-id "q-94808" :question/problem "Let $Y$ be the set of all ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$  such that $x+y\\le 3.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because **multiplication of $(x,y)\\in Y$ by a scalar is not closed.** *  \n\nFor example, $(1,1)\\in Y$ because $1+1=2\\le 3.$ However, if we add the components of $2\\cdot (1,1)=(2,2),$ we get $2+2=4>3.$ So, $2\\cdot (1,1)\\notin Y.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- The zero element in $Y$ is $(0,0).$ $✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication in $Y$ satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition in $Y$ is commutative. $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c687cc-45a6-5292-9a6d-4211b654eb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A1},\\text{A4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369998} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab86367-0fa9-543e-b61f-a4044ead541b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A1},\\text{A3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369999} {:answer/id #uuid "383e3bf7-4541-5eee-876e-909810f624cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A2},\\text{A3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370000} {:answer/id #uuid "2f464c7c-4e06-5def-b447-30f0dae4c1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A2},\\text{A4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370001} {:answer/id #uuid "29baacef-5efd-5a4e-b22e-75802a5059d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A3},\\text{A4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92c687cc-45a6-5292-9a6d-4211b654eb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A1},\\text{A4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369998} :answer-field/id #uuid "a501bedf-1c70-5820-92cf-3a7a11f55678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f54d0d72-c3d0-5096-886c-e29b889e3841" :question/math-academy-id "q-94937" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=0$, then $w=-x$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }A1\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }A4\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
